Keep num_db in step with db_list, and survive an unmapped datatype cache.

dtwm dies with SIGSEGV on address 0 while building the memory-mapped
datatype database, before it ever draws the Front Panel:

    DtActionLabel -> _DtDtsMMGet -> _DtDtsMMInit -> _DtDtsMMCreateDb
                  -> _DtDtsMMCreateFile -> _MMWriteDb -> build_new_db
                     (MMDb.c:387, `db_ptr->databaseName` with db_ptr NULL)

`db_list` is kept two ways at once: as a NULL-terminated vector, and as a
separate count in `num_db`.  `_DtDtsDbDeleteDb()` compacts the vector over
the entry it removes but never decrements the count, so after any partial
delete `num_db` is one greater than the number of live databases.
`_DtDtsMMCreateFile()` hands that count straight to `_MMWriteDb()`, which
walks `db_list[0 .. num_db-1]` and so reads the NULL terminator and
dereferences it.

The partial delete is not hypothetical — `_DtDtsMMCreateDb()` does it to
itself.  On every call after the first it runs `_DtFreeActionDB()` (which
deletes the action database) and then `_DtDtsDbAddDatabase(_DtACTION_NAME)`
to re-add it, so the count runs one further ahead of the vector per pass.

The second hunk is a safety net for the failure this used to cause.  When
`_DtDtsMMInit()` cannot map the cache it reports that by leaving `head` and
`mmaped_db` NULL, but every accessor in DtsMM.c dereferences them anyway
and faults.  Return "nothing there" — the value each caller already tests
for — so an unavailable cache degrades to the in-memory database instead of
killing the process.

diff --git a/lib/DtSvc/DtUtil1/DtsDb.c b/lib/DtSvc/DtUtil1/DtsDb.c
index 51e97a86..c9932e1a 100644
--- a/lib/DtSvc/DtUtil1/DtsDb.c
+++ b/lib/DtSvc/DtUtil1/DtsDb.c
@@ -269,6 +269,21 @@ _DtDtsDbDeleteDb(DtDtsDbDatabase *db)
 		{
 			flag = 1;
 			db_list[i] = 0;
+			/*
+			 * db_list is both NULL-terminated and separately
+			 * counted by num_db, and compacting the vector here
+			 * used to leave num_db untouched.  _DtDtsMMCreateFile
+			 * passes num_db straight to _MMWriteDb, which then
+			 * reads one entry past the last live database — the
+			 * NULL terminator — and dereferences it.  That is a
+			 * SIGSEGV on address 0, and it is how dtwm dies while
+			 * building the memory-mapped datatype database on its
+			 * way to drawing the Front Panel: _DtDtsMMCreateDb
+			 * deletes the action database and re-adds it on every
+			 * call after the first, so the count runs ahead of the
+			 * vector by one for each pass.
+			 */
+			num_db--;
 		}
 		if(flag)
 		{
